## 15. Defense Explanation Points

For your final defense, you can explain the app like this:

- The app uses **SQLite** for local task storage.
- Each task has:
  - ID
  - title
  - description
  - reminder time
  - completion status
  - created timestamp
- CRUD operations are implemented using `SQLiteOpenHelper`.
- Reminders are implemented using Android `AlarmManager`.
- When the reminder time arrives, `ReminderReceiver` receives the alarm broadcast and shows a notification.
- If the phone restarts, `BootReceiver` reschedules all pending reminders.
- The app uses a simple native Android UI built programmatically in Java.

This is a complete, runnable final-year-level Android To-Do List project.

```java
public static int secondLargest(int[] arr) {
    if (arr == null || arr.length < 2) {
        return -1;
    }

    int largest = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
    int second = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
    boolean hasSecond = false;

    for (int num : arr) {
        if (num > largest) {
            second = largest;
            largest = num;
            hasSecond = true;
        } else if (num < largest && num > second) {
            second = num;
            hasSecond = true;
        }
    }

    return hasSecond ? second : -1;
}
```

This returns the second-largest **distinct** number.

Examples:

```java
secondLargest(new int[]{5, 5, 5});      // -1
secondLargest(new int[]{});             // -1
secondLargest(new int[]{10, 20, 20});   // 10
secondLargest(new int[]{-1, -5, -3});   // -3
```

Problem: extra semicolon after the `for` loop, so the block is not part of the loop. Also `i` is scoped inside the loop.

Correct code:

```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    cout << i;
}
```

If needed with spaces:

```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    cout << i << " ";
}
